    "global": {
      "kind": "dossier optimality/uniqueness proof",
      "statement": "With $\\mu$ and $\\sigma$ free, the MLE exists if and only if the observations are not all equal; it is then unique, with $\\hat\\mu = \\bar x$ and $\\hat\\sigma^2 = n^{-1}\\sum_i (x_i - \\bar x)^2$. With $\\sigma$ held, $\\hat\\mu = \\bar x$ always. With $\\mu$ held, the MLE exists unless every observation equals $\\mu$.",
      "reason": "The normal family is a regular exponential family with sufficient statistic $(x, x^2)$ and natural parameter $(\\mu/\\sigma^2, -1/(2\\sigma^2))$. The log-likelihood is strictly concave in the natural parameter, so it has at most one maximizer, and one exists exactly when $(\\bar x, \\overline{x^2})$ lies inside the convex hull of the parabola $\\{(x, x^2)\\}$, that is when $\\overline{x^2} > \\bar x^2$; this fails only for constant samples."
